ce account  which is an identity that represents a  workload or an application application  code is most likely using a service  account  again surface accounts represent an  application or compute workload rather  than a human user a public-private RSA  key pair is used for authentication  there are no passwords so you can't log  in as a service account via the browser  service account identities also don't  belong to the Google workspace domain  there are two types of service accounts  the first is a service account that you  make for your own applications there are  also default service accounts created  when you use certain services for  example when you create a compute  instance in a brand new project the  compute engine default service account  is created and attached to the instance  for your production workloads we  recommend you create custom service  accounts tailored for the workload  rather than relying on the default  service accounts  when using a service account you have  the option to download a service account  Json file this is the private key for  the service account however we don't  recommend downloading key files if you  can avoid doing so that's because there  are some risks associated with using  service count Keys the first is  credential leakage consider the scenario  where a developer accidentally commits a  key to a public code repository that  actor can get access to the resources in  your environment using that key file  second risk is privilege escalation if a  bad actor gets access to a service  account key they might be able to use  the key to escalate their own privileges  for example the bad actor could use a  service account with permissions Google  Cloud Storage bucket to give themselves  additional privileges on the bucket the  third risk is identity masking when a  bad actor acts through a service account  key they can conceal their identity and  actions since API calls are recorded as  having been made through the service  account the main takeaway of this slide  is that anyone with the service account  Json file can make API calls as that  service account  since you now understand some risks of  using service account key files Sean  will share how we recommend you  authenticate your applications  thank you Busan as explained above there  are a lot of risk and security issues  related to using service account Keys we  are seeing a lot of customers and  developers who are authenticating with  these Keys when they should be using  alternative methods today I am going to  focus on these alternative methods as  well as what you should consider if you  must use a service account key  because there are multiple ways to  authenticate to Google Cloud's API as an  application we built out a flow diagram  to help you decide on which method to  use based on different situations  we will first go through the diagram  before jumping into the details of each  of the authentication methods  the first question that you always need  to ask yourself is whether your  application is running on Google cloud  or not this is the first part of the  flow diagram  if the application is running on Google  Cloud then you want to check are you  trying to run your application on gke or  outside of gke for gke there is a  special feature called gke workload  identity which is a simple way for you  to Federate in your kubernetes service  account to get a gcp access token if it  is not on gke then we have two methods  if you're doing development work in  local environments then we recommend  gcloud auth application default login  well if it isn't in running a local  environment then we recommend attaching  a service account directly to your  compute or serverless instances on  Google Cloud  shifting to the other side of the tree  you want to ask yourself if your app is  not running on Google cloud is it  possible to set up Federation  you are able to set up workflow and the  Federation for workloads running in  other Cloud as well as on-premise as  long as the cloud providers or your  on-premise deployment have the ability  to generate an ID token if that is not  possible then you are left with service  account key which should be the last  result I will spend the last part of  today's session going through a few  methods you can use to secure your  service account keys but before going  there I'm going to go a little more  detail into each of these authentication  methods the first method I want to focus  on is gcloud application default login  most folks who use gcloud are familiar  with gcloud auth login which allows you  to log into gcloud via the command line  this is very similar to gcloud auth  login however instead of signing in to  run methods on the command line This  Will generate a user credential for the  user who is running the command it will  then automatically put the credential in  the well-known location for application  default credentials for the client  Library which means as long as you use  client libraries in your application to  make requests to gcp API it will  automatically fetch this credential and  use it to make requests to gcp API this  is very secure and time-bound credential  which is perfect for local development  note that in this case you're not even  using a service account you're just  using the credential of your user login  now if we move away from local  development onto workflows on gcp  including serverless products like app  engine Cloud run Cloud functions or  compute products like compute engine  then the best and only way you should be  using to access gcp API is using  attached service accounts when you  create a GCE VM most times as Busan  mentioned it will automatically come  with a default service account we  recommend you to create a service  account based on applications that you  are running and replace the default  service account with this new service  account  not only will the attached service  account Grant the application ability to  authenticate without using a key the  custom created service account will also  make sure you adhere to the principle of  least privilege when it comes to calling  gcpa API you can similarly use the  client Library exactly like in the  previous slide  and in the previous example and the  client libraries will help figure out  how to retrieve the service accounts and  its permissions to perform the API  requests  we are seeing a lot of developers and  customers who are using service account  keys for the scenario especially because  Legacy tutorials floating around on the  internet  tells people to do so please make sure  to never use a service account key when  running on any of these gcp Services if  a set of other gcp resources you're  running your workloads specifically on  gke we recommend you to go check how gke  workload identity the process is  streamlined and all you need to do is  enable gke workload identity and make  sure that you have configured your  kubernetes service account inside your  clusters gke workload identity will  automatically exchange your kubernetes  token for a gcp service account token in  order to authenticate as the gcp service  account once you set this up you should  be able to allow your workload to  directly access gcp apis  finally for applications running  completely outside of Google Cloud for  example if your application is running  on AWS Azure on-prem or GitHub gitlab we  recommend you to use workload identity  Federation  as long as your workload has the ability  to generate an identity token for itself  in its native environment local identity  Federation allows you to exchange the  native ID token for gcp Access token  with the gcp access token you will be  able to impersonate a regular service  account and its permission without using  a service account key this is very  similar to the gke workflow identity  flow the only difference is that you  will have to set up the Federation pool  yourself based on whichever cloud  provider or SAS service or on-prem  service that you're trying to integrate  into gcp API  if you're using our client libraries  most of the credential exchange flow is  fully automated and taken care of by our  client libraries workload identity  Federation is very popular and much more  secure than using service account Keys  we have put in a lot of effort to make  the experience more seamless for our  customers recently we added Integrations  with gcloud gsuto and BQ CLI to enable  more use cases with these Federated  identities for customers who have apps  running on premise with on-premise  identity providers we also launched  Samuel support as well as the ability to  retrieve IDP tokens via a customer  defined executable through the client  Library this is very important if  especially if you're trying to connect  your on-premise application to gcp API  that is because each of your on-premise  identity provider might have different  ways in which to retrieve the  credentials and allowing you to write  your own executable will dramatically  simplify the process of retrieving the  credential and exchanging it for gcp  Access token  now that we went through the recommended  authentication methods there are still  scenarios where you must use a service  account key and for these scenarios I'm  going to give some recommendations to  help you best secure your service  account and your service account keys  first of all you should prefer to always  upload Keys over downloading service  account keys  this is a native feature attached to  service accounts you can use your own  pki infrastructure to generate a public  private keep here and deliver the  private Keys securely to your runtime  environment  public Keys which on the other hand are  less dangerous to be handled and passed  around can be uploaded to Google Cloud  this way there's less chance for error  when it comes to handling and passing  around private keys  secondly for scenarios where you need to  access gcp API from non-production  applications for example if you have an  intern building a proof of concept or  you're manually triggering some jobs to  retrieve data for your data SAS Services  we recently previewed the ability for  you to set up exploration time on your  service account key configuring  expiration at the project folder or  organization level will make sure that  any Keys generated under that container  will automatically have a key generated  with that the expiration time attached  note however that we don't recommend key  expiration in production environment as  it is very easy for developers to put  that into the code and when it expires  it leads to outages and that is  something that we want to help our  customers to avoid  to prevent outages I want to finally  talk about key rotation and this might  be something that you might have heard  about before but what you want to make  sure is that every developer in your  organization gets into the habit of  rotating these service count Keys due to  all the security risks that we discussed  using tools like Cloud asset inventory  you'll be able to track down when each  key has been created you can then build  custom toolings based on your  organization structure to trigger  notification to development teams to go  and rotate these Keys you can rotate you  can configure this notifications to get  louder over time if they're not act upon  alternatively you can test out tools  like terraform vault which will also  help simplify the process of rotating  these Keys although the process is  complicated if service account key is a  must in parts of your organization we  highly recommend you to set up proper  rotation policies to prevent any  security issues linked to these service  account keys long-lived service account  key is one of the biggest issues when it  comes to Cloud security and we hope this  presentation was useful for you and your  organization  thank you Busan for joining us today and  thank you all for listening and helping  to think about how we can together make  the cloud more secure  [Music]

